  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\" Reply A is the exact string: three backticks, then \"javascript\", then a newline, then {\"a\": 1}, then a newline, then three backticks, then a newline, then {\"b\": 2}. Reply B is the exact string: {\"b\": 2}. Under the rule, Reply A has a code fence holding the object {\"a\": 1}, so the required answer is {\"a\": 1}; Reply B has no fence, so the required answer is the last top-level object, {\"b\": 2}. The check, however, only recognises fences whose info string is json or JSON (or empty), because its _FENCE regex matches only triple backticks with optional json or JSON. For the javascript fence, the fenced candidate includes the word javascript and does not parse as a JSON object, so the check falls through to the top-level pool and returns {\"b\": 2} for both replies. Thus the check gives the same answer where the rule requires different answers. I would be wrong if running the check on these two replies produced different answers, or if the check returned {\"a\": 1} for Reply A."
